@startuml flow-token-lifecycle
' What happens to a session over its life: rotation, the race, theft, and the two ceilings.
'
' Render:  plantuml docs/diagrams/flow-token-lifecycle.puml

' Explicit near-white, NOT transparent: the text is dark, and a transparent background
' renders black on a dark viewer, which makes the whole diagram unreadable.
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FDFDFD
  BorderColor #5B5BD6
  ArrowColor #59606F
  DiamondBackgroundColor #EFF1F6
  DiamondBorderColor #5B5BD6
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFF8E1
  BorderColor #C2670A
}

title Refresh-token lifecycle

start

:client presents a refresh token
to POST /auth/refresh;

if (hash found in refresh_tokens?) then (no)
  #FFE0E0:401 Invalid refresh token;
  note right: Revokes nothing. A forged\ntoken is not evidence about\nanybody's real session.
  stop
endif

if (now >= sessionStartedAt + session-max?) then (yes)
  #FFE0E0:401 Session expired;
  note right
    The **absolute cap**. Each rotation pushes
    expiresAt another 30 days out, so a session
    that is merely used often enough would never
    end. sessionStartedAt is copied unchanged onto
    every successor, so this measures from when the
    user actually authenticated.

    Nothing is revoked: outliving the cap is not
    evidence of theft.
  end note
  stop
endif

if (token already revoked?) then (yes)
  if (it has a successor, rotated within refresh-grace?) then (yes)
    #E8F5E9:issue a fresh chain for the racer;
    note right
      A **rotation race**, not theft. Two tabs, or a
      reload during an in-flight refresh, both present
      the same token. Without this window the loser's
      401 would revoke the whole family — including
      the token the winner was handed milliseconds
      earlier — and sign the user out everywhere.
    end note
  else (no)
    #FFE0E0:revoke EVERY token for this user
    (RefreshTokenRevoker, REQUIRES_NEW)
    401 Refresh token reuse detected;
    note right
      REQUIRES_NEW because the 401 below would
      otherwise roll the revocation back.

      A token revoked without a successor was logged
      out, so there is no rotation to date a grace
      window from — replaying it is theft however
      fresh it looks.
    end note
    stop
  endif
else (no)
  if (expired?) then (yes)
    #FFE0E0:401 Refresh token expired;
    note right: Expiry is not theft.\nThe chain survives.
    stop
  endif
  :issue the successor
  revoke the presented token
  link it via replacedBy;
endif

:mint an access token;

note right
  Always the **ordinary** kind. The vbc claim is
  never carried across a refresh — a renewed
  session is no longer "just verified", and
  carrying it would turn a one-time proof into a
  standing permission to rewrite the password.

  sessionStartedAt is carried, unchanged.
end note

stop

@enduml
